# Line endings. The repository is authored on Windows but runs on Linux (the
# VPS, a Raspberry Pi, GitHub Actions), so the committed bytes must be LF.
#
# They already are: every tracked text blob was verified CR-free on 2026-07-28.
# That good state currently depends on each developer's `core.autocrlf`, which
# is a per-machine setting that travels with nobody. This file makes the
# guarantee explicit and portable instead of incidental.
#
# It also fixes a concrete failure: `git archive` reads these attributes from
# the tree being archived, and without them it applies the Windows checkout
# conversion — so a deploy artifact built on Windows arrives full of CRLF, and a
# shell script with CRLF fails on Linux (`\r` at end of line breaks variable
# expansion, `case` patterns, and heredoc markers). The 2026-07-28 deployment
# hit exactly that and had to strip CR on the server.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the files that actually break. Pin them explicitly.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Served and executed text — keep LF so the deployed bytes match the repo.
*.js   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
